  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23339v1</id>
    <title>Retrieve and Segment: Are a Few Examples Enough to Bridge the Supervision Gap in Open-Vocabulary Segmentation?</title>
    <updated>2026-02-26T18:45:33Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23339v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.23339v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Open-vocabulary segmentation (OVS) extends the zero-shot recognition capabilities of vision-language models (VLMs) to pixel-level prediction, enabling segmentation of arbitrary categories specified by text prompts. Despite recent progress, OVS lags behind fully supervised approaches due to two challenges: the coarse image-level supervision used to train VLMs and the semantic ambiguity of natural language. We address these limitations by introducing a few-shot setting that augments textual prompts with a support set of pixel-annotated images. Building on this, we propose a retrieval-augmented test-time adapter that learns a lightweight, per-image classifier by fusing textual and visual support features. Unlike prior methods relying on late, hand-crafted fusion, our approach performs learned, per-query fusion, achieving stronger synergy between modalities. The method supports continually expanding support sets, and applies to fine-grained tasks such as personalized segmentation. Experiments show that we significantly narrow the gap between zero-shot and supervised segmentation while preserving open-vocabulary ability.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-02-26T18:45:33Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
